#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068487 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c22b8e9-049a-5574-86c9-35788c410421" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function With an Irreducible Binomial Quadratic Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "fd2d4aca-7859-5bed-a514-43d82ba25400" :question/math-academy-id "e-4102" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor $x^2+4$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x + 3)/((x^{2} + 4)(x - 1)) &= (Ax + B)/((x^{2} + 4)) + (C)/((x - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$, $B$, and $C$, we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x^2+4)(x-1)$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3 &= (Ax + B)(x - 1) + C(x^{2} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-1, B=1$, and $C=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} = \\dfrac{-x+1}{x^2+4} +\\dfrac{1}{x-1}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(2x + 3)/((x^{2} + 4)(x - 1))dx &= ∫(\\frac{-x + 1}{x^{2} + 4} + \\frac{1}{x - 1})dx \\\\\n&= - ∫\\frac{x}{x^{2} + 4}dx + ∫\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 4}dx + ∫\\frac{1}{x - 1}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution\n\n$$\nu = x^2+4\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n2x\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{x}{x^{2} + 4}dx &= ∫(\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{du}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid u \\mid \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid x^{2} + 4 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln (x^{2} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+a^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{a}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 4}dx &= \\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{x - 1}dx &= \\ln \\mid x - 1 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(2x + 3)/((x^{2} + 4)(x - 1))dx &= - ∫\\frac{x}{x^{2} + 4}dx + ∫\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 4}dx + ∫\\frac{1}{x - 1}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln (x^{2} + 4) + \\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{2}) + \\ln \\mid x - 1 \\mid + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068513 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "116c34e0-5cfd-5bb3-964d-9f2713592207" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function With an Irreducible Trinomial Quadratic Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "3a2e21bb-c966-5429-98c0-9af209c45dfe" :question/math-academy-id "e-510" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{4x^2-x}{(x^2+x+1)(x-2)} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 1^2 - 4(1)(1) = -3 < 0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4x^{2} - x)/((x^{2} + x + 1)(x - 2)) &= \\frac{Ax + B}{x^{2} + x + 1} + \\frac{C}{x - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$, $B$, and $C$, we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x^2+x+1)(x-2)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - x &= (Ax + B)(x - 2) + C(x^{2} + x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2$, $B=1$, and $C=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x^2-x}{(x^2+x+1)(x-2)} = \\dfrac{2x+1}{x^2+x+1} + \\dfrac{2}{x-2}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration only at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(4x^{2} - x)/((x^{2} + x + 1)(x - 2))dx &= ∫(\\frac{2x + 1}{x^{2} + x + 1} + \\frac{2}{x - 2})dx \\\\\n&= ∫\\frac{2x + 1}{x^{2} + x + 1}dx + 2∫\\frac{1}{x - 2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution\n\n$$\nu = x^2+x+1\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n(2x+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{2x + 1}{x^{2} + x + 1}dx &= ∫(\\frac{1}{u})du \\\\\n&= \\ln \\mid u \\mid \\\\\n&= \\ln \\mid x^{2} + x + 1 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\ln (x^{2} + x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{x - 2}dx &= \\ln \\mid x - 2 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(4x^{2} - x)/((x^{2} + x + 1)(x - 2))dx &= ∫\\frac{2x + 1}{x^{2} + x + 1}dx + 2∫\\frac{1}{x - 2}dx \\\\\n&= \\ln (x^{2} + x + 1) + 2\\ln \\mid x - 2 \\mid + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a78860ea-192f-5df9-aca9-4f67ec6ffefe" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Given Part Of the Partial Fraction Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "8168f846-013a-529a-a916-501438b2ca30" :question/math-academy-id "e-7316" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\dfrac{4x^2-6}{(x-3)(x^2+1)} = \\dfrac{A}{x-3} + \\dfrac{x+3}{x^2 + 1}$,\nwhere $A$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{4x^2-6}{(x-3)(x^2+1)}\\,\\textrm d x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $A$. Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-3)(x^2 + 1)$ gives\n\n$$\n4x^{2} - 6 = A(x^{2} + 1) + x^{2} - 9\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(0)^{2} - 6 &= A(0^{2} + 1) + 0^{2} - 9 \\\\\n- 6 &= A - 9 \\\\\nA &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int \\dfrac{4x^2 - 6}{(x-3)(x^2+1)} \\, \\textrm{d}x & = \\int \\left(\\dfrac{3}{x-3} + \\dfrac{x+3}{x^2 + 1}\\right) \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] & = 3\\int\\dfrac{1}{x-3}\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\int \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1}\\,\\textrm d x + 3\\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2 + 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x-3} \\textrm{d}x = \\ln \\mid x-3 \\mid\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution\n\n$$\nx^2 + 1 = u\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n2x\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x & = \\int \\left(\\dfrac{1}{u}\\right) \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\vert u\\vert \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\vert x^2 + 1\\vert \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\left(x^2+1\\right). \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2 + a^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{a}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\arctan{x}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(4x^{2} - 6)/((x - 3)(x^{2} + 1))dx &= 3∫\\frac{1}{x - 3}dx + ∫\\frac{x}{x^{2} + 1}dx + 3∫\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 1}dx \\\\\n&= 3\\ln \\mid x - 3 \\mid + \\frac{1}{2}\\ln (x^{2} + 1) + 3\\arctan x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "116c34e0-5cfd-5bb3-964d-9f2713592207" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function With an Irreducible Trinomial Quadratic Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b331f0cd-7c65-5331-9813-e2eb0f9e654e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\ln \\mid x - 4 \\mid - \\frac{2}{5}\\ln (2x^{2} - 6x + 9) + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066792} {:answer/id #uuid "ddba0abc-0dbf-5883-a45a-f557fa2088f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\ln (2x^{2} - 6x + 9) - 5\\ln \\mid x - 4 \\mid + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066793} {:answer/id #uuid "2a79e75f-f0bd-557c-9790-a0eb98965684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\ln (2x^{2} - 6x + 9) - 10\\ln \\mid x - 4 \\mid + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066794} {:answer/id #uuid "37c5a9d6-9f08-56b2-8223-ff8124e1036b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\ln (2x^{2} - 6x + 9) - 5\\ln \\mid x - 4 \\mid + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066795} {:answer/id #uuid "4db1d312-bf1d-58d6-aeef-da61ae6c5d70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\ln \\mid x - 4 \\mid + \\frac{2}{5}\\ln (2x^{2} - 6x + 9) + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37c5a9d6-9f08-56b2-8223-ff8124e1036b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\ln (2x^{2} - 6x + 9) - 5\\ln \\mid x - 4 \\mid + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066795} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff70e0fb-103a-5176-9ccc-a563f1b3edb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066791}] :question/id #uuid "731b8f94-fb6c-56e8-9abe-5ac794eb1b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-3638" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nEvaluate $∫(15 - 25x)/((2x^{2} - 6x + 9)(x - 4))dx$." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c22b8e9-049a-5574-86c9-35788c410421" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function With an Irreducible Binomial Quadratic Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d484f9ee-57ab-52bd-80b7-1f50124a4ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\ln \\mid x - 1 \\mid + 2\\ln (x^{2} + 1) - \\arctan x + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067440} {:answer/id #uuid "e1407c9d-8481-5f14-8670-7336d116d474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln \\mid x - 1 \\mid + \\ln (x^{2} + 1) + \\arctan x + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067441} {:answer/id #uuid "20d92852-618a-5a8a-adca-fa9d1c995e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid x - 1 \\mid - \\frac{1}{2}\\ln (x^{2} + 1) - \\arctan x + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067442} {:answer/id #uuid "28fcb450-478d-515f-b8c4-d44949c70ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\ln \\mid x - 1 \\mid - \\frac{1}{4}\\ln (x^{2} + 1) + \\arctan x + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067443} {:answer/id #uuid "776b016d-3417-559e-9e97-bc186239e976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid x - 1 \\mid - \\ln (x^{2} + 1) + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d92852-618a-5a8a-adca-fa9d1c995e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid x - 1 \\mid - \\frac{1}{2}\\ln (x^{2} + 1) - \\arctan x + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067442} :answer-field/id #uuid "7396243b-a391-55d7-934f-905961ca56c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067439}] :question/id #uuid "d605be2d-ae71-5e50-9bf1-07465ceead87" :question/math-academy-id "q-49919" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nEvaluate $∫(2)/((x - 1)(x^{2} + 1))dx$." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
